      "dom_id": "question-3710",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14164468/assets/663096ab9ecc1daca22587d94526cbf0db841602ca0b64eaa1a9477ec8a35aee.png)\n\nWhat is the length of $a$ in the triangle shown above?",
      "worked_solution": "Here, we know the lengths of the hypotenuse and one leg, so let $b=12$ and $c=13.$ We need to find $a,$ the length of the other leg.\n\nAccording to the Pythagorean theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}^{2} & ={a}^{2}+{b}^{2} \\\\ {a}^{2} & ={c}^{2}-{b}^{2} \\\\ a & =\\sqrt{{c}^{2}-{b}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in the values for $b$ and $c$ gives us\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\sqrt{(13)^{2}-(12)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{169-144} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
